# SYNOPSIS

```usage
vxstore put store dir [tar]
vxstore tar store tree out [name=file ...]
vxstore check store tree
vxstore ls store tree [path]
vxstore cat store tree path
vxstore blocks store tree path
```

# DESCRIPTION

`vxstore` puts trees into a content store and reads them back, on the development machine, with `lib/vx-store`, the library distd(8) and install(8) use.
A store is a directory of objects, each a file named by its BLAKE2b hash under `b2/`, the first two hex digits a subdirectory: `b2/7f/7f52...`.
An object is a file's block, a file's index of its blocks, or a directory, an ndb text of its entries; a tree is named by its root directory's hash, written `b2:` and 64 hex digits (see store(6)).
build(1)'s `release` puts each architecture's base tree into `out/release/store` with it, and makes install media's stores the same way.

Modes are not the host's: a file is 0444, or 0555 if any execute bit is set; a directory is 0555 and a symbolic link 0777.
Owners and times are not kept.
So a tree's hash depends only on its names, contents and links, and two builds of one commit give one hash.
An object the store already has is not written again; a new one is written under a temporary name, then renamed.

The commands:

: `put` <store> <dir> [<tar>]
  Put the host tree under <dir> into <store>, with the entries of the ustar archive <tar>, if given, added at the tree's root (a file of the archive replaces one of <dir> with the same path).
  The store is made if it is not there.
  It prints the tree's hash and the bytes of files in it.
: `tar` <store> <tree> <out> [<name>`=`<file> ...]
  Write to <out> a ustar archive of every object <tree> reaches, each at its path in the store (`b2/xx/<hex>`), in order of path; each <file> named comes first, at the path <name>.
  build(1) uses it to make `store-ARCH.tar` and an install medium's `store.tar`, with the release's record at `records/1.ndb`.
: `check` <store> <tree>
  Read every object <tree> reaches and check it against its hash, each file's index against its size, and each block against its index.
  It exits with status 0 if all are there and sound.
: `ls` <store> <tree> [<path>]
  Print a directory of the tree, its root by default, as the directory object's text: a record for each entry, with its `name`, `mode`, and `size` and `hash` for a file, `hash` for a directory, or `link` for a link.
: `cat` <store> <tree> <path>
  Write a file's bytes to standard output, each block checked as it is read.
: `blocks` <store> <tree> <path>
  Print the store paths of a file's block objects, one a line, for tests that damage one.

# EXAMPLES

A tree of a file, an executable and a link, put in a new store and read back (the long lines are broken here, as ndb allows):

```rc
% vxstore put st tr
b2:7f52617084847c7931a877a8a79d0bc21a4a0a1d19930ae8603f7247b1a3366c 11
% t=b2:7f52617084847c7931a877a8a79d0bc21a4a0a1d19930ae8603f7247b1a3366c
% vxstore ls st $t
```

```ndb
name=README mode=0100444 size=5
    hash=b2:c0f0063dbe72505fcdaf5d90825b3f13521ed0536e18862718ff26d45e7389d5
name=bin mode=040555
    hash=b2:41f86c01ddf31899146cf094aa9a077625294786dcf1e274d6e9a99a0a419303
name=link mode=0120777 link=README
```

```rc
% vxstore cat st $t README
note
% vxstore blocks st $t README
b2/23/235d0e0671a108ae3cd0786c51cadb0cc609876cf0a3c88ba58567c199da6566
% vxstore check st $t
% vxstore tar st $t s.tar
```

